## <div style="color:#1a5276; font-size:20px; margin-bottom:10px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color:#1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color:#1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color:#1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a simple "Hello, everyone! Let's count!" while showing fingers. The teacher uses realia (one pencil, two pencils) and colorful flashcards of numbers 1-3. The teacher sings a short "Counting Song" (e.g., "One, two, three, let's count with me!") to activate prior knowledge of numbers and introduce the theme "All the Pencils!". Students listen and watch.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models counting from one to ten using finger gestures, large number flashcards (1-10), and realia (pencils, blocks, crayons). Each number is clearly pronounced, slowly and repeatedly. The teacher models the initial /k/ sound in 'count' and the initial /f/ sound in 'fingers' by exaggerating the sounds. For example, "Let's <b>c</b>ount! One <b>f</b>inger, two <b>f</b>ingers." Students listen and observe, mimicking gestures.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple visuals and gestures.
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing 3 fingers) "Is this number FIVE?" (Students listen and shake heads or say 'No').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a flashcard of number 7) "What number is this?" (Students listen and might point or attempt to say the number, or teacher prompts 'Seven!').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding up one pencil) "How many pencils?" (Students listen and might say 'One').</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, animated counting song video (e.g., "Ten Little Fingers" or "Five Little Pencils") with clear visuals and audio for numbers 1-10, and gestures. Also, large flashcards of numbers (1-10) and classroom objects (pencil, crayon, block, book, marker, glue).</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen to and recognize spoken numbers up to ten when accompanied by gestures and visuals. They will show comprehension of simple instructions in counting games.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the song about?" (Teacher guides with 'Numbers!' or 'Fingers!'). "Did you hear 'pencil'?" (Students nod or shake head).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher says a number (e.g., "Listen! Number five!"), and students point to the corresponding number on a large number line or flashcard displayed in the classroom.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Show:</b> The teacher calls out a number (e.g., "Show me three fingers!"), and students hold up the correct number of fingers using TPR.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Choose:</b> The teacher holds up two different number flashcards (e.g., 6 and 9) and says one of them (e.g., "Point to nine!"). Students listen and point to the correct card.</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Quantity + nouns ('three pencils', 'ten fingers'), Wh-questions ('How many fingers?'), Yes/No questions ('Is this number five?').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Numbers (1-10), hand, finger, pencil, crayon, marker, block, book, glue, count, show, point, hold, match, red, yellow, blue, green, orange, purple.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Initial /k/ in 'count', initial /f/ in 'fingers'. Phonemic awareness: Recognizing number sounds (1-10) and letter sounds (F, G).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Counting aloud in response to teacher prompts.</li>
        </ul></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Listen and Act):</b> The teacher plays audio recordings of numbers (1-10) and simple commands. Students listen and perform the action. Examples: "One, two, three, four, five" (students repeat chorally while counting fingers). "Hold up six fingers!" (students listen and show six fingers). "Point to the blue crayon!" (students listen and point to a blue crayon on their desk or a flashcard). The teacher emphasizes 'count' and 'fingers' for pronunciation practice.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled (Listen and Respond):</b> The teacher shows a set of objects (e.g., three pencils) and asks, "How many pencils?" Students listen and respond chorally or individually with the number (e.g., "Three!"). The teacher then asks, "Is this number five?" while showing a number 2 flashcard. Students listen and respond "No!". The teacher uses various classroom objects like blocks, books, markers, and glue.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Listen and Match (Pair Work, Guided):</b> Students work in pairs with a set of number flashcards (1-5) and object flashcards (e.g., 3 pencils, 2 blocks). One student (A) listens to the teacher saying a number or quantity (e.g., "Four pencils!"). Student A then points to the correct object flashcard, and student B confirms with a nod or 'Yes'. Then, student A points to the matching number flashcard. This activity encourages active listening and turn-taking, and prepares them for answering quantity questions.</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Hold Up Ten Fingers!"</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group "Simon Says" style counting game. Students sit in a circle or stand at their desks.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Hold Up Ten Fingers! - The Counting Game"</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher leads the game, calling out instructions using numbers and object vocabulary, reinforcing the scenario "Hold Up Ten Fingers!" and theme "All the Pencils!".</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives commands such as: "Hold up two fingers!", "Show me five fingers!", "Point to one red pencil!", "Count three blocks!" (students listen and point to or count realia on their desks), "How many hands?" (students show two hands).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher varies the commands, sometimes including colors (e.g., "Show me one yellow pencil!") and sizes (e.g., "Hold up a big block!").</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students actively listen to the teacher's instructions and demonstrate comprehension by performing the correct action (holding up fingers, pointing, counting aloud). The focus is on their ability to understand the spoken numbers and quantity instructions.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages active listening and turn-taking by sometimes asking individuals or small groups to respond.</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on individual student responses during a final 'Listen and Count' activity. The teacher will circulate and note student participation and accuracy.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Listen and Count Challenge". The teacher places various groups of classroom objects (pencils, crayons, blocks) on different tables. The teacher calls out a number and an object (e.g., "Find two red pencils!"). Students listen, walk to the correct table, and point to the specified quantity of objects. Alternatively, the teacher might say, "Show me six fingers!" to individual students.</p>
        <p style="text-align="justify"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Recognition:</b> Student consistently recognizes and points to numbers 1-10 when heard.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Following Instructions:</b> Student can follow simple counting instructions (e.g., 'Hold up five fingers', 'Point to three blocks').</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Participation:</b> Student actively participates in listening games and activities, demonstrating active listening.</li>
        </ul></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a simple drawing of two faces (happy and sad). The teacher asks: "Can you hear number 10?" (students point to happy face if yes, sad face if no). "Did you like counting today?" (students point to happy/sad face).</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new numbers did you hear today?" (Teacher facilitates by repeating numbers students heard).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you hear the /k/ sound in 'count' or the /f/ sound in 'fingers'?" (Teacher models the sound, students try to identify if they hear it).</li>
        </ul></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., TPR was effective for number recognition, the song engaged students).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for next time? (e.g., some students struggled with numbers 8-10, need more visual discrimination practice, need to vary the pacing).</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which students need extra support for listening to numbers or following instructions?</li>
        </ul></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we listened to numbers and counted. Next time, we will *look* at the numbers and *read* their names! We will see more pencils!" This sets up Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>